The original does a linear scan over every profile message on each call. This version builds a 64K-entry index table once and then answers each lookup with a single array read.

- **Same results:** Each slot keeps the first matching index, so lookups return exactly what the linear scan returned, including if a message number appears more than once.
- **Unknown numbers:** These still return `NULL`.
- **Initialization:** The table is filled inside a function-local static initializer, which C++11 and later make thread-safe.
- **Not a mutable cache:** The table is derived only from the constant `mesgs` profile data, so it does not depend on global mutable state.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static FIT_UINT16 table[65536];
    static const bool initialized = []() {
        for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
            table[k] = 0xFFFF;
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
            if (table[n] == 0xFFFF)
                table[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
        return true;
    }();
    (void)initialized;

    FIT_UINT16 idx = table[num];
    if (idx == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[idx];
}
```